A||||40||Mach10||Let's clear the water a little:||||6||
Z||000000||Mach10||09-07-2001||02:01 AM||||After a couple of PMs to me, I feel that it's time to clear up a few things about myself. <P>Anyone who's been in the same threads as myself will notice that I am very quick to leap into political arguments. After some reflection, I realize that ALL of them involved me on the side AGAINST the US.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"> <P>This unfortunately makes me look as if I have something PERSONAL against the US. Believe me, I do not. I do have a couple of sore points, which I will discuss in a moment. <P>First off, I will always jump into a Freedom of Speech argument with guns blazing. This is because I take great offence to situations where "It's my right to say this by virtue of Freedom of Speech" comes up. No, I don't want to live in China. My problem is not with the right itself, but how it is excercised. A lot of people (not all) tend to use it as a trump-card when arguing their case in a heated discussion. That is total  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"> . This conflicts horribly with what I perceive to be the underlying reason FOR this particular entry in the bill of rights. In my view, "Freedom Of Speech" came in with the idea of a completely free society of people. If you can say what you like, you aren't under any opression from the government, or anyone else. That is REALLY cool.  The problem arises when you take other people's rights into account. You are entitled to live free, and not to have to face oppression from any source, right? If this is the case, then a conflict occurs. Which right is more important? To speak your mind, or the right of another person to live without oppression? By making (for example) a rascist comment, you are belittling a person. Forcing the person underneath you. Suddenly, by excersizing your right, you are infringing on another person's. <BR>See where I'm getting at? <P>The second point of contention, is whenever a discussion of military power comes in. The most recent examples where the China-US conflict <I>in potentia</I>. I wasn't too pleased at the "Nuke 'em all, let God sort 'em out" attitude that was pervading (to a minor extent) this forum, and (to a larger extent) the media. So I launched in with a long argument about how the US could very easily lose to a drawn out assault by China. Not that it could ever happen, but hey. It's a discussion. I'm not a fan of people that radiate patriotic arrogance (regardless of nationality. In fact, chest-beaters in general piss me off...) AND no regard for human life. Scratch that. I'm not a fan of War, Period. I just raised a bunch of examples as to why attacking China would be a Bad Idea. Of course, this meant that I was belittling the US. I am sorry for that. <P>A third point, also a bit of a sore one, is the Lottery Society Phenomenon. That's right, the endless bouts of frivolous lawsuits demanding absurd amounts of money for any percieved slight. That one DOES tick me off. I can't pin this squarely on you (American) folk, however. It's starting to pop up all over the place. I still get peaved when OUR media finds the sum so unrealistically huge that they have to splatter it all over the newspapers... <P>A fourth point, is the well known American lust for scandal. Now, I will admit that it makes for some amusing headlines. I almost wet myself watching the Clinton thing... However, the lengths to which the media will spoon it out to the willing public is somewhat disturbing. If the person is known by more than a couple of people nation wide, then that person's personal life has a traget painted on it. Admittedly, there are plenty of other countries which go in for the same thing. Maybe it's a little bit of Canadian Cynicism talking here, but I do find it strange. <P>A fifth point, but not a sore one, is my comments in Starfighter's UFO/Govn't Coverup post(s). I really DID mean when I said that I found the USA's Conspiracy Theorist Sub-culture fascinating. What can I say? I don't think that people are foolish for trusting the government, but the degree to which people elaborate stories to explain odd happenings is enthralling. <P><BR>So, having said all the things that irritate/puzzle me about the US, I should now list a few things that I really like about the US... <P>First, With the possible exception of some European countries, the US is one of the more friendlier places to visit. Minimal Beaurocracy (at least for CDN tourists  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ), and very friendly people make for an invariably pleasant stay. <P>Second, the people are almost always very open and friendly. Unlike Canada, where people tend to stay off to themselves, I've seldom been in the states where someone's sidled up to me in a friendly way and pointed out that they didn't think I was from around there, and wanting to know where I was from. For truly evil and degenerate behavior, you have to travel deep into the cities, away from the more popular tourist sites. Like everywhere else in the world. <P>Third, you people are almost always terminal optimists. At least the ones I've met personally. How else can you explain Capitalism? Capitalism is the embodiment of optimism, no matter how misguided  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>Fourth, for better or worse, you people will always stand up for what you believe in, sometimes to the point of dying for it. Admittedly, I'm not a fan of most of the USs military ventures. However, I have great respect for people willing to lay down their lives for a good cause. The US has done this several times. US soldiers fight hard, and die hard. Even if I don't agree with what's going on, Bravery is a virtue, and I do respect that. <P>Does that make things a little clearer? <BR>||||reg||6
Z||000001||Black88GT||09-07-2001||02:16 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>By making (for example) a rascist comment, you are belittling a person. Forcing the person underneath you. Suddenly, by excersizing your right, you are infringing on another person's. <BR>See where I'm getting at?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>So, a person has the RIGHT not to feel belittled?  If you say so.<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>A fourth point, is the well known American lust for scandal.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What country doesn't lust for scandal?  Ever heard of the Royal Family?  Every country has a "lust" for scandal.  Maybe not Canada but everywhere else.<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>Admittedly, I'm not a fan of most of the USs military ventures.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE> <P>I bet you were a fan when we took care of the Japs for you, or the Nazi's for that matter.  While the Nazi's was more of a global endevour, I wonder how many Canadians stormed the beaches of Normandy?  I'm no historian but my guess is more Americans were there.<P>Just something to think about.<P>edited to fix quotes<P><p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 09-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Urchin||09-07-2001||02:16 AM||||Well... how long did it take you to type that out?  j/k    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Seriously though, that was well said, and I would have to say that I am in agreement with everything you said (everything that I can remember)  ...though, it appears you may take some heat for a few specifics... <P>As far as a person having a right not to "feel" belittled (feel being the key word).  The word feel was not used, and in my opinion that changes things a great deal.  You do have the right not to be subject to racist (the example used)comments, or sexual comments, etc. <P><BR>And as far as the "lust for scandel", You(Black88GT) said "maybe not in Canada",  well that is the point right there (IMO)<BR>Not trying to start a war (nice pun eh?), just my thoughts....<BR>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/side_cut_sig_4.jpg">    <BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.<P>[This message has been edited by Urchin (edited 09-07-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Urchin (edited 09-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||09-07-2001||02:41 AM||||Black88GT: In order:<BR>1) I might be digging through our own charter of Rights and Freedoms, but yes.<P>2) Yes, I agreed. However, few countries seem to take it to such an extent. Then again, this is my own personal findings. Bear in mind that if I see it this way, then there are others that do also. It's all about appearances  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Oh, and the Royal what?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>3) I wasn't born then, BUT: "taking care of the Japs" Oh, you mean char-broiling several hundred thousand <B><I>CIVILIANS</I></B>in 1/10th of a second, and dooming several more hundred thousand to agonizing death by radiation poisoning? I guess that was pretty much taken care of.<BR>Why did the US take so long to join in against Hitler? We were at it for quite a while before you got there. The US didn't win WW-II alone. Don't ever forget that. It was the ALLIED forces, not the American army.<P>And for the beaches? Does the name "Juno Beach" sound familiar? It was more fortified and heavily armed than Omaha. The Canadian soldiers nabbed (sustaining heavy losses) it while General Bradley was busy crying for reinforcements. Need the names of the regiments that we sent in? The Canadian regiments were the ones pressing inland digging trenches in front of your army. Numbers mean that much? Remember that we'd been at war for a while. We didn't have that much left, and we still did it. You guys were still fresh.<P>||||reg||4
Z||000004||OcalaFiero||09-07-2001||09:08 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>  .<P>  [QUOTE][b]Admittedly, I'm not a fan of most of the USs military ventures.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE> <P>I bet you were a fan when we took care of the Japs for you, or the Nazi's for that matter.  While the Nazi's was more of a global endevour, I wonder how many Canadians stormed the beaches of Normandy?  I'm no historian but my guess is more Americans were there.<P>Just something to think about.<P><BR>[/B][/QUOTE]<P>OK, I was going to stay out of this one, but here goes. I hold dual citizenship (Canadian/US), as I was born in Canada and later was adopted by American parents. I think of my self as both Canadian and American and even went so far as to join the US Navy after high school. So I feel that I am fairly objective here. This attitude that a lot of US citizens have, you know the John Wayne way of reminding others (Canada, Great Britain etc.) that the US "bailed them out in dubya dubya two, otherwise they'd be speaking German" is pretty lame. The US is and, for about 100 years, has been a major "superpower" in the world, but has gotten to that point because of the allies it has. In all of the "skirmishes" and "police actions" that the US seems to get involved in, it relies a great deal on the coalitions that it needs to develop. The most visible example of this is the Gulf War. Without the support that of the allied coalition, there is no way that the US could have gone into the region, let alone go into Kuwait and remove the Iraqis. Oh, and by the way, the US didn't take care of the Japanese or Germans for the Canadians, the Canadians never would have been involved in the Pacific theater against the Japanese had it not been for the US being bombed at Pearl Harbor. Also, with Canada being a British commonwealth, there were many Canadians that died on that beach (Normandy) with and for their American compatriots. Just a final thought, but with the election of Lil' Bush, the US seems to be slipping into an isolationist mentality, hope we don't burn all of our bridges in case we need some help again in the future....    <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||stimpy||09-07-2001||09:19 AM||||"Let's clear te water a little bit."  <BR>Interesting choice of title for your thread. You know how you clear te water? Leave it the F alone! You don't clear it up by sloshing it all around. No souls are gonna be saved here, no nationalities converted, no national policies changed. God, I'm getting tired of flame for flames' sake. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||09-07-2001||09:30 AM||||I'm proud to be an American citizen, but before anyone starts beating their chests saying we bailed people out in WWII, don't forget how we got into the war.  All through Hitler's Blitzkreig, the US maintained a policy of "it's not our problem" and refused to get involved.  We didn't get involved in the War in Europe until AFTER we declared war on Japan - and that was only due to Pearl Harbor.  It took a direct attack on us to get us to do anything.<P>Now, as for the Gulf War...I'm sure we wouln't have had a problem doing everything we did completely by ourselves.  But that's solely on a military level.  The coalition was vital to making sure the involved people WANTED this to happen.  It was necessary for us to get the support from the other nations so it didn't become a case of the US against Iraq.  It was a united group of nations arrayed against Iraq.  That was more of a political move than a military one.<P>As for Freedom of Speech, you have the right to express your views and speak freely to the point that it would injure another person.  That's why hate speech is not protected.  And that's why you can't run into a theatre and yell, "Fire!" ||||reg||6
Z||000007||thomas_l||09-07-2001||09:35 AM||||It's always interesting to hear the the impressions of a "visitor" and see the difference in perspective. Two things I found sort of amusing:<P>1. Lust for scandal. Well, yes. Living in the US I would say from my perspective it's more a case of Americans being nosey. From my visits to Canada, I think Canadians are kinda nosey too - just not as blatant. Look in any small community or neighborhood newspaper in the US and there is always a "police blotter" section you can read to see which of your neighbors got arrested last week. This is also why folks in the US pick up on you being a little different (probably the Canadian accent) and shamelessly ask about it. When I was in Edmonton last month some of my new in-laws finally admitted they enjoyed getting us (US folks) into discussions just to hear the accents and different perspectives. Again, just as nosey, but not as obvoius about it.<P>2. Friendly people in the US? Wow! I thought the same thing about Canadians. Some are some aren't. My immediate thought was my experience while shopping for a camera tripod at London Drug at the West Edmonton Mall. Within 15 seconds of arriving in the camera department, a salesman walked up (yes! he came to me and I didn't need to stand in line and beg for help!). He was not pushy, seemed to know everything about tripods, recommended a mid-low priced model for my use, and even had to make a special trip to the storage room because the display was empty. I was flabbergasted! Great service! Even at a A&W drive thru the cashier at the window was pleasant and helpful. When I'm at home the best I can hope for from retail sales folks is that dead stare that says "I hate my job I hate you why are you even looking my way". The only attention I would get at the local Walgreens is if I spent too much time in the store and they thought I might be a shoplifter. Even when I was returning home, the grumpy US customs folks at the airport made me feel all homesick.<P>It's all good stuff. I definitely look forward to travelling to other places and seeing how different cultures view the same things.||||reg||1
Z||000008||thomas_l||09-07-2001||09:47 AM||||Definitely some interesting perspectives on WWII. I suppose if the Japanese fleet had bigger gas tanks and flew north to attack Vancouver things might have been a little different  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. This will probably get some flames, but the war that occurred in the US from 1861-5 has two different names and most battles did too. It was perspective. Ask someone in NY and it's the Civil War. Ask the same question in Tennessee and it was the War of Northern Aggression.<P>More about perspectives: I find it very interesting how ignorant most folks are about their neighboring country (US/Canada). Last year I participated in training some customer service reps that would be supporting people in the SE US. We literally had to spend half a day doing a lesson about the culture in various southern states to give them an idea of what to expect. Likewise, I think most Americans would have trouble naming all the provinces, or even just the ones on the border  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||JSocha||09-07-2001||09:48 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||spideyfoot||09-07-2001||10:48 AM||||We are all Earthlings. Use the internet to bond with people all over the world NOT fight with them. Imagine how cool it would have been to talk to an Austrailion or Canadian for practically free 20 years ago. Heck I remember my Uncle getting all excited cause he got someone a few states away on his Ham radio. OH no spellcheck doesn't work.<p>[This message has been edited by spideyfoot (edited 09-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||FieroMaster88||09-07-2001||01:03 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"><P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroMaster88.gif">            <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>88 Coupe With a new V6<BR>5 speed Isuzu trans<BR>AcCel 8mm wires<BR>rapid fire plugs<BR>custom intake with K&N filter.<BR>Soon to be 2" closer to the ground and have a turbo||||reg||1
Z||000012||maryjane||09-07-2001||01:20 PM||||This is gonna be a little long!<BR>I'll preface this with the statement that WW-II was a WORLD war and was won by many allied nations sticking together. Losses were terribly high but the alternatives were much more terrible if you are a freedom loving people.<BR>At the time of WWII, the population of Canada was much less than today, much of the country still pristine wilderness (do you Canadiens kinda wish it still was?) the Battle of Britian had been raging for several years, losses were high. Dark years indeed. By the time DDay rolled around, Canada was indeed running out of men to fill uniforms. Remember, there was still a threat from the Japanese to invade North America.(more imagined than real, it turns out). Both the US and Canada had to maintain a defensive force at home for such an emergency. British and Canadien forces were far flung, from Africa to Hong Kong.   <BR>Regarding the comments posted about WW II, specifically Operation Overlord.<BR>The beach assigned to Canada's forces (Juno) was actually the "easiest" to invade. It was less fortified, with less high ground to climb than the contrasting Ohmaha bloodbath.<BR>The avenue out of Juno was much more open and prevented the bottleneck on the beach encountered elsewhere at Normandy. The Canucks did their job well, they just didn't face the obstacles that were present at Ohmaha. Canada's losses June 6-11 1017 KIA<BR>1814 WIA<BR>Having said that, Let's go back in time to an event called the Raid on Dieppe. It's not talked about much here in the States, but was a very important event, none the less. This was an intell gathering and propaganda mission to harrass German forces and to give a boost to the Allied forces, particlarly the British people that had been taking it on the chin at home for so many years. 6100 troops were involved:<BR>2000-Canada<BR>4050 Britian<BR>50-US<BR>Altho the raid was deemed a failure, in terms of victory or defeat, the intell gathered made the Normandy invasion possible.<BR>Without it, losses would have been far above the 15% actual (10,000 first day projected).<BR>Canada lost 907 men at Dieppe.<BR>Some of the battles Canadiens were involved in:<BR>Battle of Britain<BR>Raid on Dieppe<BR>Battle of the Bulge<BR>Burma campaign<BR>Defence of Singapore<BR>Battle of Midway<BR>Defence of Philipine Islands(There were British and Canadiens on the Bataan death march)<BR>Losses in WWII<BR>Canada-39,000<BR>US-295,000<BR>Britain-326,000<BR>Russia-over 7,000,000<BR>Sorry if I mis-spelled "Canadien" Just wanted to help set things straight there, Mach10. Sorry to all about this being so long, I'll take the rest of it to PM.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fieronv||09-07-2001||04:03 PM||||"I wasn't born then, BUT: "taking care of the Japs" Oh, you mean char-broiling several hundred thousand CIVILIANSin 1/10th of a second, and dooming several more hundred thousand to agonizing death by radiation poisoning? I guess that was pretty much taken care of."<P><BR>Mach10:<BR>I usually don't get in to the whole "who's country's is better" thing but I hate mis-imformation. The reason the US used the Atomic bomb on Japan was because of the mind set of the Japanese people during the war. The Japanese were fantical in the belief that they were superior to all others and they could not lose the war (to the point of suiciding before surrender). Also that it was dishonorable for them to surrender. On Okinawa many civilians suicided rather than surrendering to American and Austrailian forces (look up suicide cliff). It was proven that the only way to end the war with as few casualties as possible there had to be a demonstration of a weapon that made it clear that there was no hope of continuing the war. In the end the Atomic bombs probably save more lives (on both sides)than they took. <P>Also the Japanese killed more helpless civilians in China (Rape of Nanking), Korea, and Southeast Asia. The Americans could not of won WWII without the help of all the Allies (British, Austrailians, Candians, and the rest). <BR>I have studied the history of WWII for over twenty years with an open mind about who was at fault for the conflict and have found many atrocities on all side (Interment camps, Japanese unit 731, mass killing of POW's, Bataan death march, etc, etc...). <P>I find it strange that so many people have a mis-conception on the use of the atomic bombs in WWII when with a little research you can find out all the pro's and con's that went into descion for it's use. Please before making a statement like that do the research.||||reg||1
Z||000014||JohnnyK||09-07-2001||04:06 PM||||Thats true (about the beaches). I kind of remember our soldiers getting through while America got massacred..  <p>[This message has been edited by JohnnyK (edited 09-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Patrick's Dad||09-07-2001||08:12 PM||||thomas: &lt;quote&gt;...my experience while shopping for a camera tripod at London Drug at the West Edmonton Mall. Within 15 seconds of arriving in the camera department, a salesman walked up (yes! he came to me and I didn't need to stand in line and beg for help!). He was not pushy, seemed to know everything about tripods, recommended a mid-low priced model for my use, and even had to make a special trip to the storage room because the display was empty. I was flabbergasted! Great service! &lt;/quote&gt;<P>(As a camera retailer) Ouch! Things must really be tough down there in GA, now that Ritz has bought Wolf Camera....<P>The Bill of Rights was written in a different time, with people of a different mindset. Most naturally did not step on the sensibilities of others (unfortunately, yes, we did enslave others). Today, although there are no actual slaves, we have enslaved ourselves to a mindset that we have a right to do or say whatever we want, as long as it fits into the politically correct worldview. God (oops) forbid I should be pro-life (oops), or believe in traditional families (oops), or the government has no right to my money (oops), or....||||reg||4
Z||000016||frontal lobe||09-07-2001||08:17 PM||||JohnnyK, I hope I am misinterpreting your comment and smiley face, because there is NO circumstance where there can be ANY amount of humor found in the Normandy invasion and who's side (Canadian or American) got through it better.<P>There is NO room for real humans MASSACRED and a winking smiley face on the same line.<P>Mach10, I appreciate your comments.  We are BOTH fortunate to have GREAT places to live in.  Not perfect, and with some differences, but still both great places to live, work, raise families, etc.  And I always find the views of someone outside the U.S., looking in, interesting.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000017||JohnnyK||09-07-2001||08:52 PM||||Frontal: Oh GOD MAN! Yes you are misinterpreting my comment!! You guys have to stop looking for things to argue with me about. I meant our troops were better trained, because someone said the Americans were. ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Black88GT||09-07-2001||09:36 PM||||OK, does anyone actually read what I write before you go shooting your mouth off about how all Americans feel they saved the world (evil, evil Americans!)?<P>I specifically put in there that the European front was a <B>GLOBAL ENDEVOUR</B>, exact words.  Global meaning more then just the U.S.  Say it with me, "global endevour"<P>However, The Canadians/British/French/etc. really didn't have much interest in the Pacific front.  The Japs didn't bomb their major naval port.  They bombed ours.  So we were fighting a war on 2 fronts while everyone else could focus on either the European front OR the Pacific front.  However, it was in Canada's interest to support us with the Pacific front because, if we go down, Canada is coming with us.<P>In reference to our horrible slayings of civilians at Hiroshima and Nagasaki.  You say civilians, while they were civilians, they were major manufacturing cities.  So technically "civilians", but when your building planes and supplying other weapons to kill the enemy, well you make the call.<P>I would have gladly taken the deaths of "civilians" at Hiroshima and Nagasaki to end the war, especially since both of my grandfathers got orders to go to the Pacific front after Germany surrendered.  They went there for a possible invasion.  An invasion that would have caused horrible casualties, worse then any Pacific island we captured along the way. <P>Its funny how misinformed your statement is about us getting into the war late.  Canada did the same thing we did until Germany started picking on France.  Canada was doing the same things we were, supplying Europe for the war until eventually they had no choice but to join.  America did the same thing.  Remember, you not only need soldiers to fight wars, you need MONEY and SUPPLIES, which America provided plenty until they finally joined in 1941 after the Japs bombed Pearl Harbor.  So while we weren't exactly fighting we weren't just sitting back and twiddiling our thumbs either.<P>Also, like I believe someone else pointed out, your views about the atomic bomb would be very different if it was your country they originally bombed and your country who would be the ones invading.<P>So before you go misconstruing anything I have said, re-read it a few times (this goes for anyone who thinks I feel that America was the only country involved in the victory in Europe)<P>Finally, I respect the fact that Canadians fought and died in World War 2 along with every other country involved.  However, the smaller the country, the smaller the involvement (while maybe not the smaller the sacrifice - I'll give you that).<P>p.s. to johnny k and his little immature post (surprise surprise), I have a friend who's grandfather was killed in the invasion, and well, if he heard you say what you typed with a smile on your face, lets just say we would witness another massacre. <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||artherd||09-07-2001||09:45 PM||||<BR>Yeah, war really isn't funny to anyone involved with it.<P>Me? I'm an Earthling. I hate all non-Earthlings, wanna make somethin of it?!<P>(PS: Earthlings rule, come get some Aliens!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by frontal lobe:<BR><B>JohnnyK, I hope I am misinterpreting your comment and smiley face, because there is NO circumstance where there can be ANY amount of humor found in the Normandy invasion and who's side (Canadian or American) got through it better.<P>There is NO room for real humans MASSACRED and a winking smiley face on the same line.<P>Mach10, I appreciate your comments.  We are BOTH fortunate to have GREAT places to live in.  Not perfect, and with some differences, but still both great places to live, work, raise families, etc.  And I always find the views of someone outside the U.S., looking in, interesting.  Thanks.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000020||JohnnyK||09-07-2001||10:52 PM||||Immature post? Uh huh.. You said canadian troops weren't there, I said they were. Black88gt: Obviously you misinterpretted my post (surprise surprise)||||reg||1
Z||000021||Urchin||09-07-2001||11:47 PM||||Not that this is any of my business, but I really doubt JohnnyK was intentionally making light of the topic at hand.  Do you really think that he finds the idea of war/people dieing comical.  I doubt it.  I think we should all relax a little rather than picking fights over a smile being included in a post...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/side_cut_sig_4.jpg">  <BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||1
