Z||000036||frontal lobe||09-09-2001||01:54 AM||||I'm just asking because I don't know:  due to Canada's relationship with Britain, was Canada OBLIGATED to be in the war?  Any kind of a required draft or anything?  (And IF Canada WAS REQUIRED to be involved in the war, it doesn't make the sacrifice any less noble in my opinion--just so that is clear).||||reg||1
Z||000037||Mach10||09-09-2001||02:16 AM||||FL: I'm not so sure it was REQUIRED at that point, having been a sovereign nation since the 1800-somethings (Too late to remember history  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> ). However, Britain was an Ally, and we were part of the Commonwealth, so I think it was at least a moral obligation. I could be wrong. We may have signed a treaty somewhere guaranteeing military support. <BR>In anycase, our soldiers went willingly. Some even cheerfully. We still feel the loss, and pride at their bravery. <P>A perfectly valid question. You didn't have to explain  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000038||Tonker||09-09-2001||11:46 PM||||Canada was a sovereign country following the First World War. Embarassingly enough, I forget exactly how it happened. As a member of the Commonwealth and still being very much tied to the crown there was a certain moral obligation  within english Canada to join the war to protect Britain. Britain had no authority to force Canada to go to war at that point though. I forget how long the delay was between Britain and Canada declaring war - I remember it was three "something", either days or weeks. I beilieve all the recruiting initially was volountary, I don't recall if we enacted conscription or not in WW2.||||reg||1
Z||000039||Tonker||09-09-2001||11:53 PM||||I should probably clarify, I think Mach10 is right. I believe Canada was sovereign in terms of her national policies after Confederation( Constitution Act, 1867 - Don't worry  I had to crack open a textbook to look it up too, Mach  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">), but Britain controlled her International policy until WW1. After WW1, I believe Canada was given control of her own International policy by the Crown.||||reg||1
Z||000040||Mach10||09-10-2001||02:42 AM||||Tonk: We conscripted for both wars. Not much of a problem with draft-dodgers. Seems that every able-bodied Cannuck wanted a peice of Hitler...||||reg||7
